+<div class='crumb'><a href='/'>◐ Off-By-One</a> · answer catalog</div><h1>python-chacha20-ietf-poly1305-detached-tag-exactness</h1><div class='meta'><span class='badge'>1 answer(s)</span><span class='badge g'>python</span><span class='badge v'>python3</span></div><div class='desc'><p>The failure class here is not one bug but a set of independent, easy-to-get-wrong details that make an otherwise working ChaCha20-Poly1305 implementation produce tags that fail only on some inputs (empty plaintext, aligned/un-aligned AAD, partial final block). The recurring root causes are:</p></div><div class='src'><a href='https://github.com/totalwindupflightsystems/off-by-one/blob/master/data/answers/2962-python-chacha20-ietf-poly1305-detached-tag-exactness.json'>📦 Source in repository (JSON)</a></div><div class='answer'><h2>Answer</h2><h1>Fixing RFC 8439 ChaCha20-Poly1305 Detached-Tag Exactness in Pure Python</h1>
+<h2>Root-cause analysis</h2>
+<p>The failure class here is not one bug but a set of independent, easy-to-get-wrong details that make an otherwise working ChaCha20-Poly1305 implementation produce tags that fail only on <em>some</em> inputs (empty plaintext, aligned/un-aligned AAD, partial final block). The recurring root causes are:</p>
+<ol>
+<li>
+<p><strong>Wrong construction / counter.</strong> RFC 8439 uses the <em>IETF</em> variant: a <strong>96-bit (12-byte) nonce</strong> and a <strong>32-bit block counter</strong>. The Poly1305 one-time key is <code>ChaCha20(key, counter=0, nonce)[:32]</code>; encryption starts at <strong>counter = 1</strong>, not 0. Using the original 8-byte-nonce/64-bit-counter layout, or starting encryption at counter 0, corrupts both keystream and tag.</p>
+</li>
+<li>
+<p><strong>Poly1305 padding is a single <code>0x01</code> byte, appended to the block, with the block read little-endian.</strong> <code>n = int.from_bytes(block + b'\x01', 'little')</code>. Doing <code>block + b'\x01' + b'\x00'*15</code>, placing <code>0x01</code> before the bytes, or big-endian parsing silently breaks the MAC arithmetic. A short final block must still get the <code>1</code> at bit position <code>8*len(block)</code>, not <code>2^128</code>.</p>
+</li>
+<li>
+<p><strong><code>pad16</code> applied incorrectly.</strong> The AEAD MAC input is
+   <code>aad ‖ pad16(aad) ‖ ciphertext ‖ pad16(ct) ‖ le64(len(aad)) ‖ le64(len(ct))</code>,
+   where <code>pad16</code> adds <strong><code>(16 - len % 16) % 16</code></strong> zero bytes. The <code>% 16</code> matters: an already-aligned field must add <strong>zero</strong> bytes, not a full 16-byte block.</p>
+</li>
+<li>
+<p><strong>Partial final keystream block.</strong> The last XOR must use only the first <code>len(data) % 64</code> bytes of the generated block. Slicing the data and <code>zip</code>-ing with the keystream handles this; generating/padding the plaintext does not.</p>
+</li>
+<li>
+<p><strong>Tag comparison leaks.</strong> <code>expected == tag</code> (and <code>hmac.compare_digest</code> aside) short-circuits on the first differing byte, leaking the offset. Verification must fold <strong>all</strong> byte differences into one accumulator and compare only at the end — and must verify the tag <strong>before</strong> decrypting/returning plaintext.</p>
+</li>
+<li>
+<p><strong>Performance.</strong> A naive-but-correct big-integer Poly1305 must still process 4 KiB in well under 200 ms. Full 128-bit modular reduction per block is fast enough in CPython.</p>
+</li>
+</ol>
+<h2>Exact fix</h2>
+<p>Two files. <code>chacha20poly1305.py</code> is the solution; <code>test_chacha20poly1305.py</code> verifies it. Neither imports <code>cryptography</code> or <code>nacl</code>.</p>
+<h3><code>chacha20poly1305.py</code></h3>
+<pre><code class="language-python">&quot;&quot;&quot;Pure-Python RFC 8439 ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD.
+
+Implements the IETF variant (96-bit nonce, 32-bit block counter) with
+constant-time tag verification.  No third-party cryptographic packages.
+&quot;&quot;&quot;
+
+import struct
+
+MASK32 = 0xFFFFFFFF
+_CONSTANTS = (0x61707865, 0x3320646E, 0x79622D32, 0x6B206574)  # &quot;expand 32-byte k&quot;
+_P1305 = (1 &lt;&lt; 130) - 5
+_CLAMP = 0x0FFFFFFC0FFFFFFC0FFFFFFC0FFFFFFF
+
+
+def _rotl(v, n):
+    return ((v &lt;&lt; n) | (v &gt;&gt; (32 - n))) &amp; MASK32
+
+
+def _quarter_round(s, a, b, c, d):
+    s[a] = (s[a] + s[b]) &amp; MASK32
+    s[d] = _rotl(s[d] ^ s[a], 16)
+    s[c] = (s[c] + s[d]) &amp; MASK32
+    s[b] = _rotl(s[b] ^ s[c], 12)
+    s[a] = (s[a] + s[b]) &amp; MASK32
+    s[d] = _rotl(s[d] ^ s[a], 8)
+    s[c] = (s[c] + s[d]) &amp; MASK32
+    s[b] = _rotl(s[b] ^ s[c], 7)
+
+
+def chacha20_block(key, counter, nonce):
+    &quot;&quot;&quot;Return one 64-byte ChaCha20 keystream block.
+
+    key: 32 bytes, nonce: 12 bytes, counter: 32-bit integer.
+    &quot;&quot;&quot;
+    k = struct.unpack('&lt;8I', key)
+    n = struct.unpack('&lt;3I', nonce)
+    st = list(_CONSTANTS) + list(k) + [counter &amp; MASK32] + list(n)
+    w = st[:]
+    for _ in range(10):  # 10 double rounds == 20 rounds
+        _quarter_round(w, 0, 4, 8, 12)
+        _quarter_round(w, 1, 5, 9, 13)
+        _quarter_round(w, 2, 6, 10, 14)
+        _quarter_round(w, 3, 7, 11, 15)
+        _quarter_round(w, 0, 5, 10, 15)
+        _quarter_round(w, 1, 6, 11, 12)
+        _quarter_round(w, 2, 7, 8, 13)
+        _quarter_round(w, 3, 4, 9, 14)
+    return struct.pack('&lt;16I', *[(w[i] + st[i]) &amp; MASK32 for i in range(16)])
+
+
+def chacha20_encrypt(key, counter, nonce, data):
+    &quot;&quot;&quot;XOR data with the ChaCha20 keystream; handles partial final blocks.&quot;&quot;&quot;
+    out = bytearray(len(data))
+    for off in range(0, len(data), 64):
+        ks = chacha20_block(key, counter + off // 64, nonce)
+        chunk = data[off:off + 64]
+        out[off:off + len(chunk)] = bytes(x ^ y for x, y in zip(chunk, ks))
+    return bytes(out)
+
+
+def poly1305_mac(msg, key):
+    &quot;&quot;&quot;Poly1305 one-time authenticator (16-byte tag).&quot;&quot;&quot;
+    r = int.from_bytes(key[:16], 'little') &amp; _CLAMP
+    s = int.from_bytes(key[16:32], 'little')
+    acc = 0
+    for i in range(0, len(msg), 16):
+        block = msg[i:i + 16]
+        # Append the 0x01 padding byte; little-endian makes it the top byte.
+        n = int.from_bytes(block + b'\x01', 'little')
+        acc = ((acc + n) * r) % _P1305
+    return ((acc + s) &amp; ((1 &lt;&lt; 128) - 1)).to_bytes(16, 'little')
+
+
+def _pad16(b):
+    return b'\x00' * ((16 - len(b) % 16) % 16)
+
+
+def poly1305_key_gen(key, nonce):
+    return chacha20_block(key, 0, nonce)[:32]
+
+
+def _poly_input(aad, ciphertext):
+    return (aad + _pad16(aad) + ciphertext + _pad16(ciphertext)
+            + struct.pack('&lt;QQ', len(aad), len(ciphertext)))
+
+
+def _ct_equal(a, b):
+    &quot;&quot;&quot;Constant-time comparison that never reveals the first differing byte.&quot;&quot;&quot;
+    if len(a) != len(b):
+        return False
+    diff = 0
+    for x, y in zip(a, b):
+        diff |= x ^ y
+    return diff == 0
+
+
+def aead_encrypt(key, nonce, plaintext, aad=b''):
+    otk = poly1305_key_gen(key, nonce)
+    ct = chacha20_encrypt(key, 1, nonce, plaintext)
+    tag = poly1305_mac(_poly_input(aad, ct), otk)
+    return ct, tag
+
+
+def aead_decrypt(key, nonce, ciphertext, tag, aad=b''):
+    otk = poly1305_key_gen(key, nonce)
+    expected = poly1305_mac(_poly_input(aad, ciphertext), otk)
+    if not _ct_equal(expected, tag):
+        raise ValueError(&quot;authentication failed&quot;)
+    return chacha20_encrypt(key, 1, nonce, ciphertext)
+</code></pre>
+<h3><code>test_chacha20poly1305.py</code></h3>
+<pre><code class="language-python">import time
+import chacha20poly1305 as c
+
+# --- RFC 8439 Section 2.3.2: ChaCha20 block function ---
+key = bytes(range(0x00, 0x20))
+nonce = bytes.fromhex('000000090000004a00000000')
+expected_block = bytes.fromhex(
+    '10f1e7e4d13b5915500fdd1fa32071c4'
+    'c7d1f4c733c068030422aa9ac3d46c4e'
+    'd2826446079faa0914c2d705d98b02a2'
+    'b5129cd1de164eb9cbd083e8a2503c4e')
+assert c.chacha20_block(key, 1, nonce) == expected_block
+print(&quot;PASS: RFC 8439 2.3.2 block function&quot;)
+
+# --- RFC 8439 Section 2.5.2: Poly1305 ---
+pkey = bytes.fromhex(
+    '85d6be7857556d337f4452fe42d506a8'
+    '0103808afb0db2fd4abff6af4149f51b')
+assert c.poly1305_mac(b'Cryptographic Forum Research Group', pkey) == \
+    bytes.fromhex('a8061dc1305136c6c22b8baf0c0127a9')
+print(&quot;PASS: RFC 8439 2.5.2 Poly1305&quot;)
+
+# --- RFC 8439 Section 2.8.2: AEAD ---
+aead_key = bytes.fromhex(
+    '808182838485868788898a8b8c8d8e8f'
+    '909192939495969798999a9b9c9d9e9f')
+aad = bytes.fromhex('50515253c0c1c2c3c4c5c6c7')
+nonce = bytes.fromhex('07000000') + bytes.fromhex('4041424344454647')
+pt = (b&quot;Ladies and Gentlemen of the class of '99: If I could offer you &quot;
+      b&quot;only one tip for the future, sunscreen would be it.&quot;)
+expected_ct = bytes.fromhex(
+    'd31a8d34648e60db7b86afbc53ef7ec2'
+    'a4aded51296e08fea9e2b5a736ee62d6'
+    '3dbea45e8ca9671282fafb69da92728b'
+    '1a71de0a9e060b2905d6a5b67ecd3b36'
+    '92ddbd7f2d778b8c9803aee328091b58'
+    'fab324e4fad675945585808b4831d7bc'
