+<h1>R*-tree with STR Bulk Loading, Forced Reinsertion, and Level-Balanced Deletes</h1>
+<h2>Root-cause analysis</h2>
+<p>The hard parts that sink naive implementations:</p>
+<ol>
+<li><strong>Overflow always splits.</strong> R<em> requires </em>forced reinsertion first<em>: on a node's first overflow at a given height, drop <code>round(0.3·M)</code> entries farthest (by center) from the node's MBR center, then reinsert them </em><em>at their original height</em>*. Only re-split if still overfull. Must be limited to once per level per insertion.</li>
+<li><strong>Wrong split axis.</strong> R<em> picks the axis by the </em><em>sum of margins over all legal distributions</em><em>, for </em>both<em> lower- and upper-edge sortings; then, on that axis, picks the distribution minimizing </em><em>overlap</em>* (ties → area).</li>
+<li><strong>Reinsertion level.</strong> Leaf items return to level 0; internal children return to the height of the node they were removed from — not to a leaf.</li>
+<li><strong>Delete condensation.</strong> Bottom-up: drop underfull children, orphan their entries at the child's height, reinsert; promote a single-child root; reset an emptied root to a leaf; flatten orphans if the tree shrank below their height.</li>
+<li><strong>Degenerate geometry.</strong> Duplicate points/zero-area boxes force <code>area = margin = overlap = 0</code>; all comparators need stable tie-breaks and no division.</li>
+<li><strong>kNN lower bound.</strong> Use distance from query point to the <strong>subtree MBR</strong> (0 if inside), not center distance. A size-<code>k</code> max-heap plus best-first expansion gives the correct stop condition.</li>
+<li><strong>Weak validation.</strong> Must check each entry intersects <strong>every ancestor MBR</strong>, plus union-MBR equality, height consistency, fill bounds, and single leaf depth.</li>
+</ol>
+<h2>Exact fix (key mechanics)</h2>
+<p>The complete no-dependency module is embedded in <code>SOLUTION.md</code>. Core pieces:</p>
+<pre><code class="language-js">// STR: sort by x, slice, sort each slice by y, then split into P balanced groups,
+// recursing on parent MBRs until one root.
+_bulkLoad(items) {
+  if (items.length &lt;= this.M) return this._makeNode(items);
+  const P = Math.ceil(N / M), S = Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(P));
+  /* x-sort -&gt; ceil(N/S) slices -&gt; y-sort each slice -&gt; concatenate */
+  /* split into P groups of size floor(N/P) or +1 (guarantees [m, M] fill) */
+  return this._bulkLoad(parents);
+}
+
+// R* split: axis by min margin sum over lower+upper sortings, then min overlap, then area.
+_splitNode(node) { /* ... */ }
+
+// R* overflow: reinsert 30% farthest once per level, otherwise split.
+if (node.entries.length &gt; this.M) {
+  if (allowReinsert &amp;&amp; !this._reinserted.has(node.height)) {
+    const p = Math.max(1, Math.round(this.M * this.reinsertFraction));
+    const removed = this._pickFarthest(node, p);
+    for (const e of removed) queue.push({ entry: e, level: node.height });
+    if (node.entries.length &gt; this.M) return this._splitNode(node);
+    return null;
+  }
+  return this._splitNode(node);
+}
+</code></pre>
+<p>Choose-subtree uses <strong>overlap enlargement → area enlargement → area</strong> when picking a leaf; <strong>area enlargement → area</strong> for internal targets. <code>search</code> uses MBR-intersection pruning; <code>knn</code> uses a min-heap of MBR lower bounds and a size-<code>k</code> max-heap. <code>checkInvariants()</code> walks every entry against all ancestor MBRs; <code>levelStats()</code> reports per-level <code>{area, margin, overlap}</code>.</p>
